# A rescue or annotate callback is entered with the err itself, so a group
# the callback hands that err to sees a failure and refuses it, the way a
# bare binder refuses every failure. The interpreter always did this. Native
# trusted inference, which seeded every lambda parameter as never failing
# because an ordinary call refuses to hand a closure a failure, and the
# words are the two callers that hand one over on purpose. So `lam`'s entry
# guard was left out and the err walked into its body.
#
# `when_failed` is std's arm, so it reads what came out of each chain: the
# failure that passed through `lam`, on every engine.
import "std/testing"

fn mine _
  err "mine"

fn lam e
  "took {e.reason}"

fn passed r
  "passed lam, reason {r}"

fn outcome false
  "the callback's group took the failure"

fn outcome v
  v

pub play =
  print (outcome (testing/when_failed (mine 1 .? (e -> lam e)) passed))
  >> print (outcome (testing/when_failed (mine 1 .! (e -> lam e)) passed))
